# 4. PRECISE WORD-COUNTING RULES

This section is critical. Implement counting in the actual successful learning-result path, not through UI clicks or component mounting.

## 4.1 New Intake counting

A word counts toward the Daily New Intake Goal only when all of the following are true:

- It is processed through the existing New Intake mode.
- It is a genuine previously unpractised vocabulary item under the app's existing definition of a new word.
- The learner completes the existing answer/evaluation flow for that word.
- The completion is recorded successfully by the application.

Count a qualifying word at most once toward the daily New Intake goal.

Important edge cases:

- Merely opening a card does not count.
- Revealing an answer without completing the qualifying interaction does not count.
- Repeating the same word during the same day does not increment the daily unique-word count more than once.
- An incorrect answer must not be counted as a newly learned word if the existing product semantics treat that word as not successfully completed.
- An accepted typo must follow the existing evaluator's semantics. Do not invent a new answer-acceptance rule.
- If a new word is encountered in a different mode, do not automatically count it toward New Intake.
- Reviewing an already-attempted word must not count as new intake.
- The goal must not cause the intake queue to skip, reorder or repeat words incorrectly.

Inspect the current answer flow to determine exactly when a New Intake word should qualify as completed. Use that existing successful-completion boundary and document it.

If the existing product has no clear concept of successful completion for New Intake, implement the narrowest consistent rule and explain it explicitly.

## 4.2 Weak Words counting

A word counts toward the Daily Weak Words Practice Goal only when:

- It is selected through the existing Weak Words practice mode or queue.
- It is a valid member of the Weak Words queue when the qualifying practice interaction is completed.
- The existing answer/evaluation interaction has actually completed and its result has been recorded.

Count a qualifying word at most once per local calendar day toward this goal.

Important edge cases:

- Opening the Weak Words page does not count.
- Revealing an answer alone does not count.
- Repeating the same weak word multiple times on the same day does not increase the distinct-word count more than once.
- A word practised through New Intake must not count toward Weak Words merely because it later becomes weak.
- A word practised through the Due Review queue must not automatically count toward Weak Words.
- If a word ceases to be weak during the session, follow a clearly defined, consistent rule based on its qualifying status when the practice interaction is completed.
- Do not change mastery or SRS calculations merely to satisfy a daily goal.

Use stable word IDs, not French display strings or array indexes, to prevent accidental double counting.

